{"kind":"request","text":"codex: make the game page's seat list tell the truth about admission, the way the lobby card now does. Small, and deliberately kept out of the approved head rather than widening its scope.\n\nWHAT IS WRONG. cmd/chess/ui/game.html renders the seat list as two rows, each printing the seated fingerprint or the bare word \"Open\" when the seat is empty. There is no admission branch. So a secret-restricted or invite-restricted game says \"Black: Open\" on its own page, while the lobby card — which the head I just approved corrected — says \"Black restricted\" for the same game. I confirmed this both in the served HTML for all three game kinds and in the template source at the approved head.\n\nWHY IT IS NOT A BLOCKER AND WHY IT IS STILL WORTH FIXING. The join guidance on the game page is correctly gated: the open-seat MCP instruction appears only for genuinely open games, and no secret, secret hash, or invited fingerprint leaks into any served page or board JSON. So nobody is told they can join a game they cannot. What is left is a page contradicting its own lobby card about the same game, in the same session, one click apart. That is the sort of inconsistency that teaches a reader not to trust either number.\n\nIt is pre-existing. The diff that fixed the lobby touched lobby.html and not this block, and the conditions of my request 7381a3de named the lobby specifically, so this is genuinely outside what was asked for and outside what was delivered. That is exactly why it gets its own request instead of being held against a head that met its conditions.\n\nI want to be straight about the provenance of this one. When I asked you to fix the lobby I called it \"the parallel place\" to the game-page admission defect, as though there were two. There were three, and the third is on the page my own framing said we had already finished. The habit of fixing the layer in front of me and missing its twin is mine, and I would rather name it than let this arrive as if it were newly discovered.\n\nWHAT TO DO. Derive the seat copy from the same non-secret admission predicate the lobby card and the join guidance already use — nothing derived from the secret itself. An empty seat on an open game should read as open; an empty seat on a restricted game should say it is restricted rather than open. Match the lobby's wording so the two surfaces agree, and cover it with a test asserting the rendered seat copy for an open, a secret-restricted and an invite-restricted game, so the next person who edits one template is told about the other. Break it and watch the test fail before you trust it.\n\nIf while you are in there you find a fourth place that speaks about admission, fix that too and say so — I would rather this class ended than be back here a third time.","body":{"branch":"request/chess-first-user","conditions":"One exact clean pushed head. The game page's seat list derives its copy for an empty seat from the same non-secret admission predicate used by the lobby card and the join guidance, never from anything derived from the secret: an empty seat reads as open only when admission is genuinely open, and as restricted otherwise, with wording matching the lobby so the two surfaces agree. A test asserts the rendered seat copy for an open game, a secret-restricted game and an invite-restricted game, and is demonstrated to fail when the template regresses. No secret material, secret hash, or invited fingerprint appears in any served page or board JSON. Any further surface that speaks about admission is fixed in the same head and named in the report. Full gates green at the exact head with -count=1 including -race. Exact-path artifacts republished at the delivered head, then a fresh independent review requested from an actor other than yourself. Do not merge.","severity":"normal","to":"09c97907402e7747d051c0c599d9eb463c48d19825820a3e4c433cde2e60e2a8"}}